  ('cs_andrew-jackson_sec1', 'ent_andrew-jackson', 'career', 'Early career', 'An 1846 wood engraving by William Croome depicting Jackson''s marriage to Rachel Donelson in the 1847 Pictorial History of Andrew Jackson by John Frost Legal career and marriage Further information: Robards–Donelson–Jackson relationship controversy and Andrew Jackson and land speculation After the American Revolutionary War, Jackson worked as a saddler, [ 21 ] briefly returned to school, and taught reading and writing to children. [ 22 ] In 1784, he left the Waxhaws region for Salisbury, North Carolina , where he studied law under attorney Spruce Macay. [ 23 ] He completed his training under John Stokes , [ 24 ] and was admitted to the North Carolina bar in September 1787. [ 25 ] Shortly thereafter, his friend John McNairy helped him get appointed as a prosecuting attorney in the Western District of North Carolina, [ 26 ] which would later become the state of Tennessee . While traveling to assume his new position, Jackson stopped in Jonesborough . While there, he bought his first slave, Nancy, who was around his age. [ 27 ] He also fought his first duel , accusing another lawyer, Waightstill Avery , of impugning his character. The duel ended with both men firing in the air. [ 28 ] Jackson began his new career in the frontier town of Nashville in 1788 and quickly moved up in social status . [ 29 ] He became a protégé of William Blount , one of the most powerful men in the territory. [ 30 ] Jackson was appointed attorney general of the Mero District in 1791 and judge-advocate for the militia the following year. [ 31 ] He also got involved in land speculation , [ 32 ] eventually forming a partnership with fellow lawyer John Overton . [ 33 ] Their partnership mainly dealt with claims made under a "land grab" act of 1783 that opened Cherokee ( Tsalagi , ᏣᎳᎩ ) and Chickasaw ( Chikashsha ) territory to North Carolina''s white residents. [ 34 ] Jackson also became a slave trader , [ 35 ] transporting enslaved people for the interregional slave market between Nashville and t',
