  ('cs_ariana-grande_sec1', 'ent_ariana-grande', 'career', 'Career', '2008–2013: Career beginnings and Nickelodeon .mw-parser-output .hatnote{font-style:italic}.mw-parser-output div.hatnote{padding-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.5em}.mw-parser-output .hatnote i{font-style:normal}.mw-parser-output .hatnote+span.mw-empty-elt+.hatnote,.mw-parser-output .hatnote+link+.hatnote{margin-top:-0.5em}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .hatnote{display:none!important}} Main articles: Victorious and Sam & Cat Grande at the 2010 Tribeca Film Festival When she first arrived in Los Angeles , California, to meet with her managers, she expressed a desire to record an R&B album: "I was like, ''I want to make an R&B album.'' They were like ''Um, that''s a helluva goal! Who is going to buy a 14-year-old''s R&B album?! '' " [ 5 ] In 2008, Grande was cast as cheerleader Charlotte in the Broadway musical 13 . [ 15 ] [ 16 ] Grande was cast in the Nickelodeon television show Victorious along with 13 co-star Elizabeth Gillies in 2009. [ 17 ] In the sitcom, set at a performing arts high school, she played the "adorably dimwitted" Cat Valentine . [ 6 ] [ 17 ] She had to dye her hair red every other week for the role, which damaged it. [ 18 ] The show premiered in March 2010 to the second-largest audience for a live-action series in Nickelodeon, with 5.7 million viewers. [ 19 ] [ 20 ] The role helped propel Grande to teen idol status, but she was more interested in a music career, saying that acting is "fun, but music has always been first and foremost with me." [ 21 ] After the first season of Victorious wrapped, Grande wanted to focus on her music career and began working on her debut album in August 2010. [ 22 ] The second season premiered in April 2011 to 6.2 million viewers, becoming the show''s highest-rated episode. [ 23 ] In May 2011, Grande appeared in Greyson Chance ''s video for the song "Unfriend You" from his album Hold On ''til the Night (2011), portraying his ex-girlfriend. She made her first musical appearance on the track "Give It Up" from the V',
