  ('cs_barbara-bush_sec2', 'ent_barbara-bush', 'narrative', 'Second Lady of the United States (1981–1989)', 'The Bushes with UK prime minister Margaret Thatcher and Denis Thatcher at Chequers , 1984 Upon becoming vice president and second lady, the Bushes moved into the vice presidential residence in Washington. They lived there for the full eight years of George''s tenure as vice president, longer than in any of their previous homes. [ 70 ] They renovated the house, and Barbara hosted more than one thousand social events there in her time as second lady. [ 71 ] She often ignored order of precedence so that individuals would not be regularly seated among the same group, and she would sometimes have important guests sit next to her husband instead of by her. [ 72 ] First Lady Nancy Reagan grew to dislike the Bushes. During the 1980 primary election, Nancy and Barbara developed an animosity that lasted for the rest of their lives. [ 69 ] Nancy, responsible for organizing social events as first lady, reduced the social role of the vice president and the second lady. Because of this, Barbara did not take an active role in White House social events. [ 73 ] Bush joined several associations and programs to promote literacy, her preferred social cause, though she rejected more public positions so as not to overshadow Nancy Reagan. [ 74 ] [ 75 ] Bush and her initiatives in this area saw public approval. [ 76 ] She received many letters from the public, of which her white hair become such a common subject that she began using a stock reply: "Please forget about my hair. Think about my wonderful mind." [ 77 ] She also traveled extensively in the United States and abroad, both with her husband and alone while representing him. By the end of her eight years as second lady, Barbara counted 65 different nations that she had visited. [ 62 ] Bush campaigned for her husband''s reelection as vice president in the 1984 presidential campaign . [ 76 ] By the mid-1980s, Bush was comfortable speaking in front of groups, and she routinely spoke to promote issues in which she believed. She became f',
   '["clm_wiki_barbara-bush_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   80, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 353);
