  ('cs_brad-pitt_sec1', 'ent_brad-pitt', 'career', 'Career', 'Early work (1987–1993) While struggling to establish himself in Los Angeles , Pitt took lessons from acting coach Roy London . [ 17 ] [ 22 ] His acting career began in 1987, with uncredited parts in the films No Way Out (1987), No Man''s Land (1987) and Less than Zero (1987). [ 17 ] [ 23 ] In May 1987, he made his television debut in a two-episode role on the NBC soap opera Another World . [ 24 ] In November of the same year, Pitt had a guest appearance on the CBS sitcom Trial and Error [ 25 ] [ 26 ] and the ABC sitcom Growing Pains . [ 27 ] He appeared in four episodes of the CBS primetime series Dallas between December 1987 and February 1988 as Randy, the boyfriend of Jenna Wade ''s daughter Charlotte (played by Shalane McCall ). [ 28 ] Later in 1988, Pitt made a guest appearance on the Fox police drama 21 Jump Street [ 29 ] and appeared in a Pringles commercial directed by Lawrence Bridges . [ 30 ] In the same year, the Yugoslavian–American co-production The Dark Side of the Sun (1988) was his first leading film role, starring as a young American taken by his family to the Adriatic to find a remedy for a skin condition. The film was shelved at the outbreak of the Croatian War of Independence , and was not released until 1997. [ 17 ] Pitt made two motion picture appearances in 1989: the first in a supporting role in the comedy Happy Together ; the second a featured role in the horror film Cutting Class , the first of Pitt''s films to reach theaters. [ 27 ] He made guest appearances on television series Head of the Class , Freddy''s Nightmares , Thirtysomething , and (for a second time) Growing Pains . [ 31 ] Pitt was cast as Billy Canton, a drug addict who takes advantage of a young runaway (played by Juliette Lewis ) in the 1990 NBC television movie Too Young to Die? , the story of an abused teenager sentenced to death for a murder. Ken Tucker of Entertainment Weekly wrote: "Pitt is a magnificent slimeball as her hoody boyfriend; looking and sounding like a malev',
