  ('cs_charlie-chaplin_sec1', 'ent_charlie-chaplin', 'narrative', 'Filmmaking', 'Influences Chaplin believed his first influence to be his mother, who entertained him as a child by sitting at the window and mimicking passers-by. He once stated "it was through watching her that I learned not only how to express emotions with my hands and face, but also how to observe and study people". [ 369 ] Chaplin''s early years in music hall allowed him to see stage comedians at work, he also attended the Christmas pantomimes at Drury Lane , where he studied the art of clowning through performers like Dan Leno . [ 370 ] Chaplin''s years with the Fred Karno company had a formative effect on him as an actor and filmmaker. Simon Louvish writes that the company was his "training ground", [ 371 ] and it was here that Chaplin learned to vary the pace of his comedy. [ 372 ] The concept of mixing pathos with slapstick was learnt from Karno, [ al ] who also used elements of absurdity that became familiar in Chaplin''s gags. [ 372 ] [ 373 ] From the film industry, Chaplin drew upon the work of the French comedian Max Linder , whose films he greatly admired. [ 374 ] In developing the Tramp costume and persona, he was likely inspired by the American vaudeville scene, where tramp characters were common. [ 375 ] Method A 1922 image of Charlie Chaplin Studios , where all of Chaplin''s films between 1918 and 1952 were produced Chaplin never spoke more than cursorily about his filmmaking methods, claiming such a thing would be tantamount to a magician spoiling his own illusion. [ 376 ] Little was known about his working process throughout his lifetime, [ 377 ] but research from film historians – particularly the findings of Kevin Brownlow and David Gill that were presented in the three-part documentary Unknown Chaplin (1983) – has since revealed his unique working method. [ 378 ] Until he began making spoken dialogue films with The Great Dictator (1940), Chaplin never shot from a completed script. [ 379 ] Many of his early films began with only a vague premise, for example "',
   '["clm_wiki_charlie-chaplin_sec1"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   80, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 357);
