  ('cs_charlie-chaplin_sec3', 'ent_charlie-chaplin', 'narrative', 'Awards and nominations', 'Star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame at 6755 Hollywood Boulevard Chaplin received many awards and honours, especially later in life. In the 1975 New Year Honours , he was appointed a Knight Commander of the Order of the British Empire (KBE). [ 443 ] He was also awarded honorary Doctor of Letters degrees by the University of Oxford and the University of Durham in 1962. [ 336 ] In 1965, he and Ingmar Bergman were joint winners of the Erasmus Prize [ 444 ] and, in 1971, he was appointed a Commander of the National Order of the Legion of Honour by the French government. [ 445 ] From the film industry, Chaplin received a special Golden Lion at the Venice Film Festival in 1972, [ 446 ] and a Lifetime Achievement Award from the Lincoln Center Film Society the same year. The latter has since been presented annually to filmmakers as The Chaplin Award. [ 447 ] Chaplin was given a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame in 1972, having been previously excluded because of his political beliefs. [ 448 ] Chaplin received three Academy Awards : an Honorary Award for "versatility and genius in acting, writing, directing, and producing The Circus " in 1929 , [ 193 ] a second Honorary Award for "the incalculable effect he has had in making motion pictures the art form of this century" in 1972 , [ 352 ] and a Best Score award in 1973 for Limelight (shared with Ray Rasch and Larry Russell). [ 439 ] He was further nominated in the Best Actor , Best Original Screenplay , and Best Picture (as producer) categories for The Great Dictator , and received another Best Original Screenplay nomination for Monsieur Verdoux . [ 449 ] In 1976, Chaplin was made a Fellow of the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA). [ 450 ] Six of Chaplin''s films have been selected for preservation in the National Film Registry by the United States Library of Congress : The Immigrant (1917), The Kid (1921), The Gold Rush (1925), City Lights (1931), Modern Times (1936), and The Great Dictator (1940). [ 451 ] Mo',
   '["clm_wiki_charlie-chaplin_sec3"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   80, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 363);
UPDATE content_section SET body_markdown = 'Charles Parker Jr., nicknamed "Bird" or "Yardbird", was an American jazz saxophonist, bandleader, and composer. Parker was a highly influential soloist and leading figure in the development of bebop, a form of jazz characterized by fast tempos, virtuosic technique, and advanced harmonies. He was a virtuoso and introduced revolutionary rhythmic and harmonic ideas into jazz, including rapid passing chords, new variants of altered chords, and chord substitutions. Parker primarily played the alto saxophone.', word_count = 73, last_verified_at = unixepoch() WHERE id = 'cs_charlie-parker_intro';
