  ('cs_clarence-thomas_sec1', 'ent_clarence-thomas', 'career', 'Early legal career', 'Thomas is sworn in as Assistant Secretary of Education for the Office for Civil Rights in 1981. With no job offers from major law firms, Thomas took a position as an associate with Missouri Attorney General John Danforth , who offered him the prospect of practicing what he liked. [ 78 ] Thomas moved to Saint Louis to study for the Missouri bar , and was admitted on September 13, 1974. [ 79 ] He remained financially destitute even after leaving Yale, trying unsuccessfully on one occasion to make money by selling his blood at a blood bank , and hoped that by working for Danforth he might later acquire a job in private practice. [ 80 ] [ 81 ] From 1974 to 1977, Thomas was an assistant attorney general of Missouri—the only African-American member of Danforth''s staff. He worked first in the office''s criminal appeals division and later in the revenue and taxation division. Thomas conducted lawsuits independently, gaining a reputation as a fair but controversial prosecutor. [ 82 ] Years later, after he joined the Supreme Court, Thomas recalled his position in Missouri as "the best job I''ve ever had". [ 83 ] When Danforth was elected to the U.S. Senate in 1976, Thomas left to become an attorney in Monsanto ''s legal department in Saint Louis. [ 84 ] He found the job unsatisfying, so left to rejoin Danforth in Washington, D.C. , as a legislative assistant. [ 85 ] From 1979 to 1981, he handled energy issues for the Senate Commerce Committee . As a black conservative who had switched his party affiliation from Democratic to Republican while working for Danforth in Missouri, [ 86 ] Thomas soon drew the attention of officials in the newly elected Reagan Administration . [ 86 ] [ 87 ] [ 88 ] Pendleton James, Reagan''s personnel director, offered Thomas the position of assistant secretary for civil rights at the U.S. Department of Education . Initially reluctant, Thomas agreed after Danforth and others pressed him to take the post. [ 89 ] Thomas in his office, May 1981 On May 1',
   '["clm_wiki_clarence-thomas_sec1"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
