  ('cs_crazy-horse_sec3', 'ent_crazy-horse', 'narrative', 'Last Sun Dance of 1877', 'The Last Sun Dance of 1877 is significant in Lakota history as the Sun Dance held to honor Crazy Horse one year after the victory at the Battle of the Little Big Horn , and to offer prayers for him in the trying times ahead. Crazy Horse attended the Sun Dance as the honored guest but did not take part in the dancing. [ 79 ] Five warrior cousins sacrificed blood and flesh for Crazy Horse at the Last Sun Dance of 1877. The five warrior cousins were three brothers, Flying Hawk , Kicking Bear , and Black Fox II, all sons of Chief Black Fox, also known as Great Kicking Bear, and two other cousins, Eagle Thunder and Walking Eagle. [ 80 ] [ d ] On September 4, 1877, Young Black Fox commanded Crazy Horse''s warriors in the latter''s absence—a display of courage that earned him the regard of both Lakota and white observers alike. That same year, he sought sanctuary in Canada, but he did not survive his return to the United States, falling in 1881 at the hands of warriors from a rival tribe. [ 81 ] [ 82 ]',
