  ('cs_douglas-macarthur_sec3', 'ent_douglas-macarthur', 'narrative', 'World War I', 'Rainbow Division Brigadier General MacArthur holding a riding crop at a French château, September 1918 MacArthur returned to the War Department, where he was promoted to major on 11 December 1915. In June 1916, he was assigned as head of the Bureau of Information. MacArthur has been regarded as the Army''s first press officer. [ 34 ] Following the American entry into World War I , MacArthur and Secretary of War Newton D. Baker secured an agreement from President Wilson for the use of the National Guard on the Western Front. MacArthur suggested sending first a division organized from units of different states, so as to avoid the appearance of favoritism toward any state. Baker approved the creation of this formation, which became the 42nd ("Rainbow") Division and appointed Major General William Abram Mann as its commander; MacArthur was its chief of staff, and with his new role came the rank of colonel , skipping the rank of lieutenant colonel. [ 35 ] At MacArthur''s request, this commission was in the infantry rather than the engineers. [ 36 ] [ 37 ] The 42nd Division''s initial training emphasized open-field combat rather than trench warfare . It sailed for the Western Front on 18 October 1917. On 19 December, Mann was replaced by Major General Charles T. Menoher after Mann–who was "ill, old, and bedridden"– [ 38 ] failed a physical examination. [ 39 ] [ 40 ] The new division commander and his chief of staff "became great friends", in MacArthur''s words, who described Menoher as "an able officer, an efficient administrator, of genial disposition and unimpeachable character". [ 41 ] Lunéville-Baccarat Defensive sector French General Georges de Bazelaire decorates MacArthur with the Croix de Guerre , 18 March 1918 The 42nd Division entered the line in the quiet Lunéville sector in February 1918. On 26 February, MacArthur accompanied a French trench raid and assisted in the capture of German prisoners. The commander of the French VII Corps [ fr ] , Major General Georg',
