  ('cs_duke-ellington_sec2', 'ent_duke-ellington', 'narrative', 'Personal life', 'Mercer referred to Mildred Dixon as his mother Ellington married his high school sweetheart, Edna Thompson (1898–1967), [ 99 ] on July 2, 1918, when he was 19. [ 100 ] The next spring, on March 11, 1919, Edna gave birth to their only child, Mercer Kennedy Ellington ( died "}]],"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Abbr","href":"./Template:Abbr"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"d."},"2":{"wt":"died"}},"i":0}}]}">d. 1996). [ 100 ] Ellington was joined in New York City by his wife and son in the late 1920s, but the couple soon permanently separated. [ 101 ] According to her obituary in Jet magazine, she was "homesick for Washington" and returned. [ 99 ] In 1929, Ellington became the companion of Mildred Dixon , [ 102 ] who traveled with him; managed Tempo Music; inspired songs, such as " Sophisticated Lady ", [ 103 ] at the peak of his career; and raised his son. [ 104 ] [ 105 ] [ 106 ] In 1938, he left his family (when Mercer was 19) and moved in with Beatrice "Evie" Ellis, a Cotton Club employee. [ 107 ] Their relationship, though stormy, continued after Ellington met and formed a relationship with Fernanda de Castro Monte in the early 1960s. [ 108 ] Ellington supported both women for the rest of his life. [ 109 ] Ellington''s sister Ruth (1915–2004) later ran Tempo Music, his music publishing company. [ 106 ] Ruth''s second husband was the bass-baritone McHenry Boatwright , whom she met when he sang at her brother''s funeral. [ 110 ] As an adult, Mercer played trumpet and piano, led his own band, worked as his father''s business manager, [ 111 ] and kept the Ellington band alive for 20 years after Duke''s death. [ 112 ] Ellington was a member of Alpha Phi Alpha [ 113 ] fraternity and was a Freemason associated with Prince Hall Freemasonry . [ 114 ]',
   '["clm_wiki_duke-ellington_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   80, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 306);
