  ('cs_eleanor-roosevelt_sec2', 'ent_eleanor-roosevelt', 'narrative', 'First Lady of the United States (1933–1945)', 'Roosevelt making an appeal for the Red Cross , May 22, 1940 Roosevelt became First Lady of the United States when Franklin was inaugurated on March 4, 1933. Having known all of the twentieth century''s previous first ladies, she was seriously depressed at having to assume the role, which had traditionally been restricted to domesticity and hostessing. [ 97 ] Her immediate predecessor, Lou Henry Hoover , had ended her feminist activism on becoming first lady, stating her intention to be only a "backdrop for Bertie ." [ 98 ] Eleanor''s distress at these precedents was severe enough that Hickok subtitled her biography of Roosevelt "Reluctant First Lady". [ 99 ] With support from Howe and Hickok, Roosevelt set out to redefine the position. According to her biographer Blanche Wiesen Cook , she became "the most controversial First Lady in United States history" in the process. [ 99 ] Despite criticism of them both, with her husband''s strong support she continued with the active business and speaking agenda she had begun before assuming the role of first lady in an era when few married women had careers. She was the first presidential spouse to hold regular press conferences and in 1940 became the first to speak at a national party convention. [ 100 ] She also wrote a daily and widely syndicated newspaper column, " My Day ", another first for a presidential spouse. [ 101 ] [ 102 ] She was also the first first lady to write a monthly magazine column and to host a weekly radio show. [ 103 ] In the first year of her husband''s administration, Roosevelt was determined to match his presidential salary, and she earned $75,000 from her lectures and writing, most of which she gave to charity. [ 104 ] By 1941, she was receiving lecture fees of $1,000, [ 51 ] and was made an honorary member of Phi Beta Kappa at one of her lectures to celebrate her achievements. [ 105 ] [ 106 ] [ non-primary source needed ] Roosevelt maintained a heavy travel schedule in her twelve years in the Whit',
   '["clm_wiki_eleanor-roosevelt_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
