  ('cs_elon-musk_sec1', 'ent_elon-musk', 'career', 'Business career', 'Main article: Business career of Elon Musk Zip2 Main article: Zip2 Quick facts External videos ... External videos Musk speaks of his early business experience during a 2014 commencement speech at University of Southern California on YouTube Close In 1995, Musk, his brother Kimbal , and Greg Kouri founded Zip2 , a web software company, with funding from a group of angel investors. [ 64 ] They housed the venture at a small rented office in Palo Alto. [ 65 ] Replying to Rolling Stone , Musk denounced the notion that they started their company with funds borrowed from Musk''s father Errol Musk, [ 33 ] but in a tweet, he recognized that his father contributed 10% of a later funding round. [ 66 ] The company developed and marketed an Internet city guide for the newspaper publishing industry, with maps, directions, and yellow pages. [ 67 ] According to Musk, "The website was up during the day and I was coding it at night, seven days a week, all the time." [ 65 ] To impress investors, Musk built a large plastic structure around a standard computer to create the impression that Zip2 was powered by a small supercomputer. [ 68 ] The Musk brothers obtained contracts with The New York Times and the Chicago Tribune , [ 69 ] and persuaded the board of directors to abandon plans for a merger with CitySearch . [ 70 ] Musk''s attempts to become CEO were thwarted by the board. [ 71 ] Compaq acquired Zip2 for $307 million in cash in February 1999 ( equivalent to $590,000,000 in 2025 ), [ 72 ] [ 73 ] and Musk received $22 million ( equivalent to $43,000,000 in 2025 ) for his 7-percent share. [ 74 ] X.com and PayPal Main articles: X.com (bank) , PayPal , and PayPal Mafia In 1999, Musk co-founded X.com, an online financial services and e-mail payment company. [ 75 ] The startup was one of the first federally insured online banks, and, in its initial months of operation, over 200,000 customers joined the service. [ 76 ] The company''s investors regarded Musk as inexperienced and replaced ',
