  ('cs_emmitt-smith_sec1', 'ent_emmitt-smith', 'career', 'College career', '1987 season Smith accepted an athletic scholarship to attend the University of Florida in Gainesville , where he played for coach Galen Hall ''s Gators for three seasons ( 1987 – 1989 ). [ 19 ] He did not start the first two games of his college career in the fall of 1987, but made the most of his opportunities in a second-week rout of Tulsa in which he gained 109 yards on just ten carries, including a 66-yard touchdown run. [ 20 ] That performance earned him a spot in the starting lineup the following week in the Gators'' SEC opener against Alabama at Legion Field . In his first collegiate start, Smith promptly broke Florida''s 57-year-old all-time single game rushing record held by Red Bethea , carrying 39 times for 224 yards and two touchdowns as the Gators upset the Crimson Tide. [ 11 ] [ 21 ] Smith went on to break the 1,000-yard barrier in the seventh game of his freshman season, the fastest any running back had ever broken that barrier to begin his college career. [ 11 ] [ 22 ] [ 23 ] He finished the season with 229 carries for 1,341 yards and 13 touchdowns. He was named SEC and National Freshman of the Year, [ 19 ] and was ninth in the balloting for the Heisman Trophy . [ 24 ] [ 25 ] [ 26 ] He earned the most votes for an underclassman with the eight finishing ahead of him being seniors. [ 27 ] 1988 season The Gators got off to a 5–0 start in 1988 with Smith averaging over 120 rushing yards per game. [ 28 ] However, during the sixth contest against Memphis State in mid-October, he was sidelined with a sprained knee while starting quarterback Kyle Morris broke his finger, leading to an upset loss. [ 29 ] Smith was unable to return to action for a month, during which Florida lost four games in a row and did not score a touchdown in 14 consecutive quarters of play, including a 16–0 loss to Auburn that is their most recent shutout to date. [ 30 ] Smith returned to the lineup in mid-November and Florida won two of its final three games, highlighted by a victory i',
