  ('cs_franklin-d-roosevelt_sec1', 'ent_franklin-d-roosevelt', 'narrative', 'Marriage, family, and extramarital affairs', 'Eleanor and Franklin with their first two children, 1908 During his second year of college, Roosevelt met and proposed to Boston heiress Alice Ruggles Sohier , who turned him down. [ 25 ] Franklin then began courting his childhood acquaintance and fifth cousin once removed, Eleanor Roosevelt , a niece of Theodore Roosevelt. [ 26 ] In 1903, Franklin proposed to Eleanor. Despite "initial resistance" from his mother, Franklin and Eleanor Roosevelt were married on March 17, 1905. [ 4 ] [ 27 ] Theodore, who was then President, gave away the bride since Eleanor''s father Elliott was deceased. [ 28 ] The young couple moved into Springwood . Franklin''s mother, Sara Roosevelt, also provided a townhouse for the newlyweds in New York City, and had a house built for herself alongside that townhouse. Eleanor never felt at home in the houses at Hyde Park or New York City. However, she loved the family''s vacation home on Campobello Island , also a gift from Sara. [ 29 ] Burns indicates that young Franklin Roosevelt was self-assured and at ease in the upper class. [ 30 ] On the other hand, Eleanor was shy and insecure, and initially focused on her husband''s career. [ 31 ] As his father had done, Franklin left childcare to his wife, and Eleanor assigned the task to caregivers. [ 32 ] They had six children. Their only daughter, Anna , was born in 1906. [ 33 ] James was born in 1907. [ 34 ] The couple''s second son, Franklin, died in infancy in 1909. [ 35 ] Elliott was born in 1910, another son named Franklin followed in 1914, and the youngest, John , was born in 1916. [ 36 ] Roosevelt had multiple extramarital affairs. He commenced an affair with Eleanor''s social secretary, Lucy Mercer , soon after she was hired in 1914. That affair was discovered by Eleanor in 1918. [ 37 ] [ 38 ] Franklin contemplated divorcing Eleanor, but Sara objected, and Mercer would not marry a divorced man with five children. [ 39 ] Franklin promised never to see Mercer again, but their marriage shifted t',
   '["clm_wiki_franklin-d-roosevelt_sec1"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
