  ('cs_freddie-mercury_sec3', 'ent_freddie-mercury', 'legacy', 'Illness and death', 'Mercury exhibited HIV/AIDS symptoms as early as 1982. Authors Matt Richards and Mark Langthorne have stated in their biographical book about Mercury, Somebody to Love: The Life, Death, and Legacy of Freddie Mercury , that Mercury secretly visited a doctor in New York City to get a white lesion on his tongue checked (which might have been hairy leukoplakia , one of the first signs of an HIV infection) a few weeks before Queen''s final American appearance with Mercury on Saturday Night Live on 25 September 1982, where he began to exhibit the symptoms of someone recently infected with HIV. [ 152 ] Mountain Studios in Montreux, Switzerland, Queen''s recording studio from 1978 to 1995. Mercury recorded his final vocals here in May 1991. In December 2013, the studio was opened free as the "Queen Studio Experience", with fans asked for a donation to the Mercury Phoenix Trust charity. [ 153 ] In October 1986, two months after Mercury''s final live performance with Queen at Knebworth House on the Magic Tour , the British tabloid newspapers the News of the World and The Sun reported that he had his blood tested for HIV/AIDS at a Harley Street clinic, but he was quoted as saying he was "perfectly fit and healthy". [ 154 ] According to his partner, Jim Hutton, Mercury was diagnosed with AIDS in late April 1987. [ 155 ] Around that time, he said in an interview that he had tested negative for HIV. [ 37 ] He told his Queen bandmates of his illness in May 1989. [ 156 ] The British press pursued rumours about Mercury''s health during the final years of his life, fuelled by his increasingly gaunt appearance, Queen''s absence from touring, and reports from his former lovers to tabloid journalists . By 1990, rumours about his health were rife. [ 157 ] Mercury and his inner circle of colleagues and friends continually denied the stories. It has been suggested that he could have helped AIDS awareness by speaking earlier about his illness. [ 52 ] [ 158 ] At the 1990 Brit Awards held at t',
   '["clm_wiki_freddie-mercury_sec3"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   90, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 354);
UPDATE content_section SET body_markdown = 'Frederick Douglass was an American social reformer, abolitionist, orator, writer, and statesman. He was "the most important leader of the movement for African-American civil rights in the 19th century".', word_count = 29, last_verified_at = unixepoch() WHERE id = 'cs_frederick-douglass_intro';
