  ('cs_hubert-humphrey_sec1', 'ent_hubert-humphrey', 'career', 'Marriage and early career', 'In 1934, Humphrey began dating Muriel Buck , a bookkeeper and graduate of local Huron College. [ 29 ] They were married from 1936 until Humphrey''s death nearly 42 years later. [ 30 ] They had four children: Nancy Faye, Skip Humphrey , Robert Andrew, and Douglas Sannes. [ 31 ] Money was an issue that plagued the Humphreys consistently. One biographer noted, "For much of his life he was short of money to live on, and his relentless drive to attain the White House seemed at times like one long, losing struggle to raise enough campaign funds to get there." [ 32 ] To help boost his salary, Humphrey frequently took paid outside speaking engagements. Through most of his years as a U.S. senator and vice president, he lived in a middle-class suburban housing development in Chevy Chase, Maryland . In 1958, the Humphreys used their savings and his speaking fees to build a lakefront home in Waverly, Minnesota , about 40 miles west of Minneapolis. [ 33 ] During World War II , Humphrey tried three times to join the armed forces but failed. [ 34 ] His first two attempts were to join the Navy, first as a commissioned officer and then as an enlisted man. He was rejected both times for color blindness. [ 35 ] He then tried to enlist in the Army in December 1944 but failed the physical exam because of a double hernia, color blindness, and calcification of the lungs. [ 35 ] Despite his attempts to join the military, one biographer would note that "all through his political life, Humphrey was dogged by the charge that he was a draft dodger" during the war. [ 36 ] Humphrey led various wartime government agencies and worked as a college instructor. In 1942, he was the state director of new production training and reemployment and chief of the Minnesota war service program. [ 37 ] In 1943 he was the assistant director of the War Manpower Commission . [ 23 ] From 1943 to 1944, Humphrey was a professor of political science at Macalester College in Saint Paul, Minnesota , where he headed the',
   '["clm_wiki_hubert-humphrey_sec1"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
