  ('cs_j-cole_sec2', 'ent_j-cole', 'career', 'Basketball career', 'Cole played basketball at Terry Sanford High School in North Carolina . With an academic scholarship, he tried out as a walk-on at St. John''s University and was one of 10 call-back players during his sophomore year. [ 184 ] In 2012, Cole played for the Eastern Team during the NBA All-Star Weekend Celebrity Game . [ 185 ] In 2013, Cole told Sports Illustrated , "Sports is where it started for me. It parallels my life. Rap is such a competitive thing. That''s why I have to watch sports. I got to keep up. It''s my life in just another form." [ 186 ] On July 20, 2020, Cole released an article for The Players'' Tribune , writing about his goals after graduating college to eventually playing professional basketball saying "if I can blow up in the next three years, that means I''ll only be 27. That still might give me enough time to train and pursue a professional basketball career. I''ll work hard enough to go play overseas and then try to work my way to the NBA." [ 187 ] On July 31, 2020, Cole released his debut signature shoe in collaboration with Puma called the PUMA RS-Dreamer. [ 188 ] In August 2020, it was also reported by Master P that Cole was training to tryout for the NBA . [ 189 ] In September 2022, J. Cole was named as the cover athlete for NBA 2K23 on the Dreamer Edition , and was featured in the game, as well as a character in the game''s "MyCareer" mode. Cole said in a press release, "NBA 2K has long been a place to discover new musical talent through their game and continues to be a gold standard for showcasing all things basketball culture. It''s been an amazing journey to not only appear on a cover of this year''s game, but to be part of the MyCareer storyline, soundtrack and bring the Dreamer brand into NBA 2K." [ 190 ] [ 191 ] Patriots Basketball Club (2021) On May 10, 2021, Cole signed a contract with the Rwanda -based Patriots Basketball Club in the Basketball Africa League . [ 192 ] Cole was also featured on the cover of the American basketball mag',
