  ('cs_james-madison_sec3', 'ent_james-madison', 'narrative', 'Congressman and party leader (1789–1801)', 'Further information: Presidency of George Washington Election to Congress Main article: 1789 Virginia''s 5th congressional district election After Virginia ratified the constitution, Madison returned to New York and resumed his duties in the Congress of the Confederation. Madison unsuccessfully ran for the U.S. Senate in Virginia in 1788. [ 93 ] After Madison was defeated in his bid for the Senate, and with concerns for both his political career and the possibility that Patrick Henry and his allies would arrange for a second constitutional convention, Madison ran for the House of Representatives. [ 94 ] [ 95 ] [ 96 ] Henry and the Anti-Federalists were in firm control of the General Assembly in the autumn of 1788. [ 96 ] At Henry''s behest, the Virginia legislature designed to deny Madison a seat by gerrymandering congressional districts. Henry and his supporters ensured Orange County was in a district heavily populated with Anti-Federalists, roughly three to one, to oppose Madison. [ 96 ] [ 97 ] [ 96 ] Henry also recruited James Monroe , a strong challenger to Madison. [ 97 ] Locked in a difficult race against Monroe, Madison promised to support a series of constitutional amendments to protect individual liberties. [ 94 ] In an open letter, Madison wrote that, while he had opposed requiring alterations to the Constitution before ratification, he now believed that "amendments, if pursued with a proper moderation and in a proper mode ... may serve the double purpose of satisfying the minds of well-meaning opponents, and of providing additional guards in favor of liberty." [ 98 ] Madison''s promise paid off, as in Virginia''s 5th district election , he gained a seat in Congress with 57 percent of the vote. [ 4 ] Madison became a key adviser to Washington, who valued Madison''s understanding of the Constitution. [ 94 ] Madison helped Washington write his first inaugural address and also prepared the official House response to Washington''s speech. He played a significa',
   '["clm_wiki_james-madison_sec3"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
