VALUES
  ('cs_jerry-rice_sec3', 'ent_jerry-rice', 'legacy', 'Legacy', 'Rice (far right) at a White House ceremony honoring the San Francisco 49ers'' Super Bowl XXIII victory Rice holds numerous NFL receiving records. His 197 career touchdown receptions are 41 scores more than the second place of 156 touchdown receptions by Randy Moss ; his 208 total touchdowns (197 receiving, ten rushing, and one fumble recovery) are 33 scores ahead of Emmitt Smith ''s second-place total of 175. [ 349 ] His 22,895 career receiving yards are 5,403 yards ahead of the second-place Larry Fitzgerald . His 1,256 career points scored make him the highest-scoring non- kicker in NFL history. [ 350 ] Many of these records are considered by sports analysts to be unbreakable. [ 351 ] [ 352 ] [ 353 ] [ 354 ] [ 355 ] During a career spanning two decades, Rice averaged 75.6 receiving yards per game. [ 7 ] He received MVP votes in six out of his 20 seasons, finishing as the runner-up twice in 1987 and 1995. [ 356 ] [ 357 ] [ 358 ] He won two NFL Offensive Player of The Year Awards, and a Bert Bell Award in 1987. [ 359 ] Rice is remembered as one of the best clutch players in football history, often making game-winning catches throughout his career. Rice also was noted as an effective blocking receiver. [ 196 ] Rice''s "famous" catching ability, sometimes attributed to him having to catch bricks when working with his father during his childhood, led him to 1,549 career receptions, 117 ahead of the second-place Fitzgerald. [ 360 ] [ 361 ] Despite being keen about his public image early in his career, Rice is remembered for his work ethic and dedication. [ 362 ] He still cared extensively about his physical appearance, often tailoring his uniform and jersey, and combed his hair before putting on his helmet; his philosophy was: "If you look good, feel good, you''ll play good", as described by Shawn Rogers, one of his friends. [ 22 ] In his 20 NFL seasons, Rice missed only 17 regular season games, 14 of them in the 1997 season and the other three in the strike-shortened se',
   '["clm_wiki_jerry-rice_sec3"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   90, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 362);
UPDATE content_section SET body_markdown = 'Jesse Louis Jackson was an American civil rights activist, LGBTQ rights activist, politician, and ordained Baptist minister. A protégé of Martin Luther King Jr. and James Bevel during the civil rights movement, he became one of the most prominent civil rights leaders of the late 20th and early 21st centuries, and an ardent advocate and early supporter of LGBTQ rights in the United States. From 1991 to 1997, he served as a shadow United States senator for the District of Columbia.', word_count = 81, last_verified_at = unixepoch() WHERE id = 'cs_jesse-jackson_intro';
