  ('cs_jimmy-wales_sec3', 'ent_jimmy-wales', 'narrative', 'Personal life', 'Wales with his second wife, Christine Rohan, in 2007 Wales has been married three times. At the age of 20, he married Pamela Green, [ 147 ] a co-worker at a grocery store in Alabama. [ 67 ] They divorced in 1993. [ 8 ] He met his second wife, Christine Rohan, through a friend in Chicago while she worked as a steel trader for Mitsubishi . [ 13 ] [ 18 ] They were married in Monroe County, Florida , in March 1997, [ 148 ] and had a daughter before separating in 2008. [ 147 ] [ 13 ] [ 67 ] They divorced in 2011. [ 8 ] Wales moved to San Diego in 1998, and after becoming disillusioned with the housing market there, moved in 2002 to St. Petersburg, Florida . [ 18 ] [ 46 ] [ 149 ] He had a brief relationship with Canadian conservative columnist Rachel Marsden in 2008 that began after Marsden contacted Wales about her Wikipedia biography. [ 150 ] After accusations that Wales'' relationship constituted a conflict of interest , he stated that there had been a relationship but that it was over and that it had not influenced any matters on Wikipedia, [ 151 ] [ 152 ] a statement Marsden disputed. [ 153 ] Wales married Kate Garvey at Wesley''s Chapel in London on October 6, 2012. [ 154 ] Garvey is Tony Blair ''s former diary secretary ; the couple met in Davos , Switzerland. [ 155 ] [ 156 ] Wales has two daughters with Garvey in addition to his daughter with Rohan. [ 64 ] [ 157 ] Wales is irreligious . In an interview with Big Think , he said his philosophy is firmly rooted in reason , and that he is a complete non-believer. [ 158 ] He is a passionate cook. [ 79 ] [ 159 ] Wales has lived in London since 2012, [ 160 ] and became a British citizen in 2019. [ 161 ] In 2021, on The Tim Ferriss Show podcast, he revealed that he secretly lived in Buenos Aires , Argentina , for one month after reading Ferriss''s book The 4-Hour Workweek . [ 162 ] Wales''s book The Seven Rules of Trust was published in October 2025 by Penguin Random House . It was described by the publisher as a "sweepin',
