  ('cs_joan-mitchell_sec2', 'ent_joan-mitchell', 'career', 'Early career (New York)', 'In 1950, Mitchell saw her first Willem de Kooning painting at the Whitney Annual. [ 1 ] That year, Mitchell painted what would be her last picture with a human figure incorporated in the work Figure and the City , of which she said later "I knew that it would be my last figure". [ 26 ] Then throughout the 1950s, Mitchell was active in the New York School of artists and poets, and was associated with the American Abstract expressionist movement, [ 27 ] although she personally abhorred aesthetic labels. [ 28 ] Beginning in 1950, she maintained a studio in Greenwich Village , first on Eleventh Street and later on Ninth Street. [ 24 ] She would maintain a New York City apartment until 1982, despite being based in France starting in the late 1950s. [ 29 ] She was a regular at established artist gathering spots like the Cedar Tavern and The Club, an invitation-only loft space on Eighth Street where Mitchell participated in panel discussions and attended social gatherings throughout the 1950s. [ 23 ] : 32 Mitchell maintained a robust creative discourse with fellow New York School painters Philip Guston , Franz Kline , and Willem de Kooning , whose work she greatly admired. [ 23 ] As Katy Siegel wrote in Joan Mitchell , "She went to their studios and shows, and they came to hers; she had dinner and drinks with them, in company and alone, talking painting materials and great art." [ 23 ] : 31–32 Along with Lee Krasner , Grace Hartigan , Helen Frankenthaler , Shirley Jaffe , Elaine de Kooning , and Sonia Gechtoff , Mitchell was one of the few female artists in her era to gain critical acclaim and recognition. [ 30 ] In 1951, Mitchell''s work was exhibited in the landmark " Ninth Street Show ", organized by art dealer Leo Castelli and by members of the Artists'' Club; the show also included work by Jackson Pollock , Willem de Kooning, and Hans Hofmann . [ 31 ] Joan Mitchell carried her large abstract painting across town with the help of Castelli. [ 32 ] Her friends Kline and ',
   '["clm_wiki_joan-mitchell_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
