  ('cs_joe-montana_sec1', 'ent_joe-montana', 'career', 'College career', '1974 When Montana arrived at Notre Dame in the fall of 1974, the football program was coached by Ara Parseghian . [ 16 ] During Parseghian''s tenure, Notre Dame won the NCAA national championship in 1966 and 1973 . Parseghian''s success as a coach helped him recruit highly talented players. Though Montana was a top prospect, under Notre Dame policy in 1974 freshmen were not permitted to practice with or play on the varsity team, and consequently Montana appeared only in a few freshman team games. [ 14 ] Montana''s first significant contributions to the Notre Dame football team came during his sophomore year. On December 15, 1974, Parseghian resigned due to health problems. [ 14 ] The university hired Dan Devine to replace Parseghian. Despite his limited playing time the previous year, Montana performed well during the 1975 spring practice. Devine was so impressed that he later told his wife: "I''m gonna start Joe Montana in the final spring game." When she replied, "Who''s Joe Montana?", Devine said: "He''s the guy who''s going to feed our family for the next few years." [ 14 ] 1975 Devine did not feel Montana was ready to be the full-time starter in 1975; [ 14 ] however, Montana played a key role in Notre Dame''s victory over North Carolina . [ 14 ] During the game, played in Chapel Hill , Montana came in with 5:11 left to play. At the time, North Carolina led by a score of 14–6. Montana spent one minute and two seconds of game time on the field. In that time, he had 129 passing yards and Notre Dame won the game, 21–14. [ 14 ] Against Air Force , Notre Dame''s next opponent, Montana again entered the game in the fourth quarter. Although Air Force led 30–10, Notre Dame won the game, 31–30. [ 14 ] After the win against North Carolina, Devine said Moose Krause , the Notre Dame athletic director , said the game was the "greatest comeback I''ve ever seen". [ 14 ] After the game against Air Force, Krause was quoted as saying: "This one''s better than last week." [ 14 ] I',
