  ('cs_john-coltrane_sec2', 'ent_john-coltrane', 'narrative', 'Personal life and religious beliefs', 'Upbringing and early influences Coltrane was born and raised in a Christian home. He was influenced by religion and spirituality beginning in childhood. His maternal grandfather, the Reverend William Blair, was a minister at an African Methodist Episcopal Zion Church [ 96 ] [ 97 ] in High Point, North Carolina , and his paternal grandfather, the Reverend William H. Coltrane, was an A.M.E. Zion minister in Hamlet, North Carolina . [ 96 ] Critic Norman Weinstein observed the parallel between Coltrane''s music and his experience in the southern church, [ 98 ] which included practicing music there as a youth. First marriage In 1955, Coltrane married Naima ( née Juanita Grubbs). Naima Coltrane, a Muslim convert, heavily influenced his spirituality. When the couple married, she had a 5-year-old daughter named Antonia, later named Syeeda, whom Coltrane adopted. He met Naima at the home of bassist Steve Davis in Philadelphia. The love ballad he wrote to honor his wife, " Naima ", was Coltrane''s favorite composition. In 1956, the couple left Philadelphia with their daughter and moved to New York City. In August 1957, the three moved into an apartment on 103rd Street and Amsterdam Avenue in New York. A few years later, John and Naima purchased a home at 116–60 Mexico Street in St. Albans, Queens . [ 99 ] This is the house where they would break up in 1963. [ 100 ] About the breakup, Naima said in the biography Chasin'' the Trane by J. C. Thomas, "I could feel it was going to happen sooner or later, so I wasn''t really surprised when John moved out of the house in the summer of 1963. He didn''t offer any explanation. He just told me there were things he had to do, and he left only with his clothes and his horns. He stayed in a hotel sometimes, other times with his mother in Philadelphia. All he said was, ''Naima, I''m going to make a change.'' Even though I could feel it coming, it hurt, and I didn''t get over it for at least another year." Despite this, Coltrane kept a close',
   '["clm_wiki_john-coltrane_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
   80, unixepoch(), 'wikipedia', 355);
