  ('cs_john-lewis_sec2', 'ent_john-lewis', 'narrative', 'Field Foundation, SRC, and VEP (1966–1977)', 'In 1966, Lewis moved to New York City to take a job as the associate director of the Field Foundation of New York . [ 58 ] [ 59 ] He was there a little over a year before moving back to Atlanta to direct the Southern Regional Council ''s Community Organization Project. [ 60 ] [ 59 ] During his time with the Field Foundation, he completed his degree from Fisk University. [ 61 ] In 1970, Lewis became the director of the Voter Education Project (VEP), a position he held until 1977. [ 62 ] Though initially a project of the Southern Regional Council, the VEP became an independent organization in 1971. [ 63 ] Despite difficulties caused by the 1973–1975 recession , [ 63 ] the VEP added nearly four million minority voters to the rolls under Lewis''s leadership. [ 64 ] During his tenure, the VEP expanded its mission, including running Voter Mobilization Tours. [ 63 ]',
   '["clm_wiki_john-lewis_sec2"]', '["src_wikidata"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'kp-027-migration-0027', 'auto_approved', 1,
