  ('cs_keith-jarrett_sec1', 'ent_keith-jarrett', 'career', 'Career', 'The Jazz Messengers Main article: The Jazz Messengers In 1964, Jarrett moved to New York City , where he played at the Village Vanguard in Greenwich Village . [ 14 ] Art Blakey hired Jarrett to play with The Jazz Messengers . Jarrett''s appearance on the Messengers'' live album Buttercorn Lady (1966) marked his commercial recording debut. [ 15 ] However, there was friction between Blakey and Jarrett, and Jarrett left after four months of touring. [ 16 ] Charles Lloyd Quartet During a show, he was noticed by Jack DeJohnette , who recommended Jarrett to his band leader Charles Lloyd . [ 17 ] The Charles Lloyd Quartet had formed not long before and was exploring open, improvised forms while building supple grooves, and it was moving into terrain that was also being explored, although from another stylistic background, by some of the psychedelic rock bands of the West Coast. [ 18 ] Their 1966 album Forest Flower was one of the most successful jazz recordings of the mid-1960s. [ 19 ] They were invited to play The Fillmore in San Francisco, and won over the local hippie audience. [ 20 ] The quartet toured across the U.S. and Europe, including appearances in Leningrad and Moscow. [ 20 ] Their concert at London''s Royal Albert Hall was attended by The Beatles . [ 21 ] The band was profiled in Time and Harper''s Magazine , [ 20 ] which made Jarrett a popular musician in rock and jazz. The tour also laid the foundation for a lasting musical bond with DeJohnette. [ 18 ] Jarrett began to record his own tracks as a leader of small groups, at first in a trio with Charlie Haden and Paul Motian . Life Between the Exit Signs (1967), his first album as a band leader, was released by Vortex and was followed by Restoration Ruin (1968), which Thom Jurek of AllMusic later wrote was "a curiosity in his catalog". [ 14 ] Not only does Jarrett barely touch the piano, but he plays all the other instruments on what is essentially a folk-rock album; he also sings. [ 14 ] Somewhere Before , anot',
