  ('cs_kelly-rowland_sec1', 'ent_kelly-rowland', 'career', 'Career', '1997–2001: Destiny''s Child Taken from a passage in the Biblical Book of Isaiah , [ 21 ] the group changed their name to Destiny''s Child, after signing to Columbia Records in 1997. [ 22 ] That same year, Destiny''s Child recorded their major label debut song "Killing Time", for the soundtrack to the 1997 film Men in Black . [ 23 ] Later that year, the group released their debut single, " No, No, No ", and the following year, they released their self-titled debut album . [ 15 ] The album amassed moderate sales and won the group three Soul Train Lady of Soul Awards . The group released their multi-platinum second album The Writing''s on the Wall in 1999. [ 24 ] The album features some of the group''s most successful singles such as " Bills, Bills, Bills ", " Jumpin'' Jumpin'' " and " Say My Name ". "Say My Name" won Best R&B Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocals and Best R&B Song at the 43rd Annual Grammy Awards . [ 25 ] The Writing''s on the Wall sold more than 15 million copies worldwide. [ 15 ] [ 26 ] [ 27 ] Destiny''s Child performing their 2000 hit " Say My Name " during their farewell concert tour, Destiny Fulfilled ... And Lovin'' It Along with their commercial successes, the group became entangled in much-publicized turmoil involving the filing of a lawsuit by group members Luckett and Roberson for breach of contract. Michelle Williams and Farrah Franklin appeared in the video of "Say My Name", and Luckett and Roberson were dismissed from the group. Franklin would eventually quit the group after five months. [ 15 ] After settling on their final lineup, the trio released " Independent Women Part I ", which appeared on the soundtrack to the 2000 film, Charlie''s Angels . It became their best-charting single, topping the Billboard Hot 100 for eleven consecutive weeks. [ 26 ] That same year, Luckett and Roberson withdrew their case against their now-former bandmates, while maintaining the suit against Mathew, which ended in both sides agreeing to stop public ',
